{"success": true, "message": "Test details sent successfully", "data": {"test": {"_id": "69de21f97e39d99b57bc5b88", "analysisVideoCdn": "yt", "exam": ["605dfcb2f226ac25a8bd5fef"], "ctype": null, "position": 0, "order": 12, "enableCalculator": false, "isContinuousQuestion": false, "reattemptAvailable": false, "singleSectionMultipleSubjects": false, "timeRestricted": false, "title": "d and f Block Elements - Test 7", "description": "Previous Year Questions as Chapter Test", "syllabus": "This test contains questions from all topics of this chapter included in the JEE Main syllabus. It includes a mix of questions from PYQs of JEE Main 2019 - 2025.", "totalTime": 37, "totalQuestions": 25, "totalMarks": 100, "bonusQuestions": null, "bonusMarks": null, "startDate": "2026-04-15T03:30:00.000Z", "endDate": "2027-04-30T00:00:00.000Z", "type": "free", "class": "605df635a37b7025c438d685", "instruction": "608289ffb160b022e9442c83", "subjects": [{"subjectId": "607018ee404ae53194e73d91", "totalMarks": 100, "bonusMarks": 0, "totalQuestions": 25, "bonusQuestions": 0, "_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d924673c"}], "sections": [{"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466ea", "title": "Chemistry Single Correct", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d91", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d9246682", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f45"], "concepts": ["6117b96e67dbe2603c3e9e62"], "question": {"text": "The metal that shows highest and maximum number of oxidation state is :"}, "options": [{"id": "76dbd5", "text": "\\(\\mathrm{Fe}\\)", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d9246683"}, {"id": "3ed131", "text": "\\(\\mathrm{Mn}\\)", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d9246684"}, {"id": "9388ba", "text": "Co", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d9246685"}, {"id": "c145e8", "text": "Ti", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d9246686"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d9246687", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb2066f9ce3f243b4b73"], "concepts": ["60d3035daf049203d853666b"], "question": {"text": "<p>Given below are two statements:</p><p>Statement I : Iron (III) catalyst, acidified <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></math> and neutral <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>KMnO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math> have the ability to oxidise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math>&nbsp;to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;independently.</p><p>Statement II: Manganate ion is paramagnetic in nature and involves <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p&#960;</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>p&#960;</mi></math> bonding.</p><p>In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options.</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "d809c2", "text": "Both statement I and Statement II are ture", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d9246688"}, {"id": "d08588", "text": "Both statement I and Statement II are false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d9246689"}, {"id": "ccfddb", "text": "Statement I is true but Statement II is false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d924668a"}, {"id": "56db6d", "text": "Statement I is false but Statement II is true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d924668b"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d924668c", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f45"], "concepts": ["60e567ab499d1e49588f95dc"], "question": {"text": "In chromyl chloride, the number of d-electrons present on chromium is same as in (Given at no. of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ti</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>23</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Cr</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>24</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Mn</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>25</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Fe</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>26</mn></math>)"}, "options": [{"id": "3fe832", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>IV</mi><mo>)</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d924668d"}, {"id": "b8bfc2", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Mn</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>VII</mi><mo>)</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d924668e"}, {"id": "ad4186", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Fe</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>III</mi><mo>)</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d924668f"}, {"id": "9a9e13", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ti</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>III</mi><mo>)</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d9246690"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d9246691", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f45"], "concepts": ["60e2fb5666f9ce3f243b4f18"], "question": {"text": "Which one of the following when dissolved in water gives coloured solution in nitrogen atmosphere?"}, "options": [{"id": "5b2ef5", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Cu</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d9246692"}, {"id": "a99f0c", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>ZnCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d9246693"}, {"id": "c55277", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CuCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d9246694"}, {"id": "9c91e0", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>AgCl</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d9246695"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d9246696", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f45"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52be"], "question": {"text": "The metal ions that have the calculated spin only magnetic moment value of 4.9 B.M. are<br />A. $\\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}$<br />B. $\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$<br />C. $\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$<br />D. $\\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$<br />E. $\\mathrm{Mn}^{3+}$<br />Choose the correct answer from the options given below"}, "options": [{"id": "11acbc", "text": "A, C and E only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d9246697"}, {"id": "a97ca4", "text": "A, D and E only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d9246698"}, {"id": "5a8503", "text": "B and E only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d9246699"}, {"id": "8c97ea", "text": "A, B and E only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d924669a"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d924669b", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f45"], "concepts": ["6162fbe46ed13040ec8543c1"], "question": {"text": "Give below are two statements:\nStatement I : The higher oxidation states are more stable down the group among transition elements unlike p-block elements.\nStatement II : Copper can not liberate hydrogen from weak acids.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :"}, "options": [{"id": "6db0cc", "text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d924669c"}, {"id": "76cb92", "text": "Statement I is false but Statement II is true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d924669d"}, {"id": "f992ec", "text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d924669e"}, {"id": "ea8e27", "text": "Statement I is true but Statement II is false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d924669f"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466a0", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f45"], "concepts": ["61ab7a186fe44bfe0fe4b93c"], "question": {"text": "The major components in &quot;Gun Metal&quot; are:"}, "options": [{"id": "36912a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cu</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Sn</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Zn</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466a1"}, {"id": "61eee9", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Al</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Cu</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Mg</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Mn</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466a2"}, {"id": "2aea65", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cu</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Ni</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Fe</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466a3"}, {"id": "c63665", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cu</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Zn</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ni</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466a4"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466a5", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f45"], "concepts": ["60e567ab499d1e49588f95dc"], "question": {"text": "The transition elements that has the lowest enthalpy of atomisation is:"}, "options": [{"id": "69003a", "text": "<math><mi>V</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466a6"}, {"id": "120abc", "text": "<math><mi>F</mi><mi>e</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466a7"}, {"id": "c35bd6", "text": "<math><mi>Z</mi><mi>n</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466a8"}, {"id": "8a696e", "text": "<math><mi>C</mi><mi>u</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466a9"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466aa", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f45"], "concepts": ["6162fbe46ed13040ec8543c1"], "question": {"text": "<p>Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).<br />Assertion (A) : <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Cu</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> in water is more stable than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Cu</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math>.<br />Reason (R) : Enthalpy of hydration for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Cu</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> is much less than that of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Cu</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math>.</p><p>In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :<br />&nbsp;</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "1a4763", "text": "&nbsp;Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466ab"}, {"id": "1d7980", "text": "(A) is correct but (R) is not correct.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466ac"}, {"id": "5fcc86", "text": "(A) is not correct but (R) is correct.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466ad"}, {"id": "02f4e5", "text": "Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466ae"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466af", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f45"], "concepts": ["60e2fb5666f9ce3f243b4f18"], "question": {"text": "The orange colour of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></math> and purple colour of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>KMnO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math> is due to"}, "options": [{"id": "c4fd1a", "text": "Charge transfer transition in both.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466b0"}, {"id": "8fbd41", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></math> transition in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>KMnO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math> and charge transfer transitions in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></math>.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466b1"}, {"id": "1ee97a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></math> transition in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></math> and charge transfer transitions in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>KMnO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466b2"}, {"id": "466070", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></math> transition in both.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466b3"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466b4", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f45"], "concepts": ["60e567ab499d1e49588f95dc"], "question": {"text": "Which of the following <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>metal</mi></math> ion will give the lowest enthalpy of hydration <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#916;</mi><mi>hyd</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> when dissolved in water?"}, "options": [{"id": "2c21fe", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Cr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466b5"}, {"id": "3527b4", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Mn</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466b6"}, {"id": "3c21dc", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Fe</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466b7"}, {"id": "34fcd3", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Co</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466b8"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466b9", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f45"], "concepts": ["60e2fb5666f9ce3f243b4f18"], "question": {"text": "The set having ions which are coloured and paramagnetic both is -"}, "options": [{"id": "5a7a36", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Cu</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>Cr</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>Sc</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466ba"}, {"id": "933f33", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Cu</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>Zn</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>Mn</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466bb"}, {"id": "e4bd91", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Sc</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>Ti</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466bc"}, {"id": "bd3f0b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Ni</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>Mn</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>Hg</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466bd"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466be", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f45"], "concepts": ["6162fbe46ed13040ec8543c1"], "question": {"text": "<p>Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mfenced></math> and the other is labelled as Reason <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mfenced><mo>.</mo></math></p><p>Assertion <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mfenced></math> : In aqueous solutions <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Cr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> is reducing while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Mn</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> is oxidising in nature.</p><p>Reason <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mfenced><mo>:</mo></math> Extra stability to half filled electronic configuration is observed than incompletely filled electronic configuration.</p><p>In the light of the above statement, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "b93226", "text": "Both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mfenced></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mfenced></math> are true and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">R</mi></mfenced></math> is the correct explanation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466bf"}, {"id": "b87eab", "text": "Both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mfenced></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mfenced></math> are true but <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mfenced></math> is not the correct explanation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466c0"}, {"id": "fda005", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mfenced></math> is false but <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mfenced></math> is true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466c1"}, {"id": "2e10f4", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mfenced></math> is true but <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mfenced></math> is false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466c2"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466c3", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f45"], "concepts": ["60e567ab499d1e49588f95dc"], "question": {"text": "The correct decreasing order of spin only magnetic moment values $(\\mathrm{BM})$ of $\\mathrm{Cu}^{+}, \\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}, \\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}$ and $\\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ ions is :"}, "options": [{"id": "5d2e09", "text": "$\\mathrm{Cu}^{+} \\gt \\mathrm{Cu}^{2+} \\gt \\mathrm{Cr}^{3+} \\gt \\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466c4"}, {"id": "2c2234", "text": "$\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+} \\gt \\mathrm{Cu}^{+} \\gt \\mathrm{Cr}^{2+} \\gt \\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466c5"}, {"id": "1d9b8f", "text": "$\\mathrm{Cr}^{2+} \\gt \\mathrm{Cr}^{3+} \\gt \\mathrm{Cu}^{2+} \\gt \\mathrm{Cu}^{+}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466c6"}, {"id": "53befe", "text": "$\\mathrm{Cr}^{3+} \\gt \\mathrm{Cr}^{2+} \\gt \\mathrm{Cu}^{+} \\gt \\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466c7"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466c8", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f45"], "concepts": ["60e567ab499d1e49588f95dc"], "question": {"text": "The number of valence electrons present in the metal among $\\mathrm{Cr}, \\mathrm{Co}, \\mathrm{Fe}$ and Ni which has the lowest enthalpy of atomisation is"}, "options": [{"id": "ebd653", "text": "$8$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466c9"}, {"id": "b3e62e", "text": "$9$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466ca"}, {"id": "ba36c9", "text": "$6$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466cb"}, {"id": "edb123", "text": "$10$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466cc"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466cd", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f45"], "concepts": ["60e567ab499d1e49588f95dc"], "question": {"text": "The electrode potential of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math> d-series elements shows positive value for?"}, "options": [{"id": "a3d04b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Co</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466ce"}, {"id": "a017e0", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Fe</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466cf"}, {"id": "070ec8", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Zn</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466d0"}, {"id": "f54f35", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cu</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466d1"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466d2", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f45"], "concepts": ["60e567ab499d1e49588f95dc"], "question": {"text": "Arrange the following elements in the increasing order of number of unpaired electrons in it.\n(A) \\(\\mathrm{Sc}\\)\n(B) \\(\\mathrm{Cr}\\)\n(C) \\(\\mathrm{V}\\)\n(D) \\(\\mathrm{Ti}\\)\n(E) Mn\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :"}, "options": [{"id": "d5aadf", "text": "\\((\\mathrm{A})  < (\\mathrm{D}) < (\\mathrm{C}) < (\\mathrm{B}) < (\\mathrm{E})\\)", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466d3"}, {"id": "6245de", "text": "\\((\\mathrm{C})  < (\\mathrm{E}) < (\\mathrm{B}) < (\\mathrm{A}) < (\\mathrm{D})\\)", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466d4"}, {"id": "12737a", "text": "\\((\\mathrm{A})  < (\\mathrm{D}) < (\\mathrm{C}) < (\\mathrm{E}) < (\\mathrm{B})\\)", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466d5"}, {"id": "a7e739", "text": "\\(\\text { (B) }  < \\text { (C) } < \\text { (D) } < \\text { (E) } < \\text { (A) }\\)", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466d6"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466d7", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f45"], "concepts": ["60e567ab499d1e49588f95dc"], "question": {"text": "lectronic configuration of \\(\\mathrm{Cu}(\\mathrm{II})\\) is \\(3 \\mathrm{~d}^9\\) whereas that of \\(\\mathrm{Cu}(\\mathrm{I})\\) is \\(3 \\mathrm{~d}^{10}\\). Which of the following is correct?"}, "options": [{"id": "47368d", "text": "Stability of \\(\\mathrm{Cu}(\\mathrm{I})\\) and \\(\\mathrm{Cu}(\\mathrm{II})\\) depends on nature of copper salts", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466d8"}, {"id": "25d4d9", "text": "\\(\\mathrm{Cu}(\\mathrm{II})\\) is more stable", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466d9"}, {"id": "f20280", "text": "\\(\\mathrm{Cu}(\\mathrm{I})\\) and \\(\\mathrm{Cu}(\\mathrm{II})\\) are equally stable", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466da"}, {"id": "666572", "text": "\\(\\mathrm{Cu}\\) (II) is less stable", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466db"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466dc", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f45"], "concepts": ["60e567ab499d1e49588f95dc"], "question": {"text": "<p>Match List-I with List-II</p><table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\" cellspacing=\"1\" style=\"width: 500px;\"> <tbody> <tr> <td> </td> <td>List I (Species)</td> <td> </td> <td>List II (Electronic distribution)</td> </tr> <tr> <td>A.</td> <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Cr</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math></td> <td>i.</td> <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>8</mn></msup></math></td> </tr> <tr> <td>B.</td> <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Mn</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math></td> <td>ii.</td> <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></math></td> </tr> <tr> <td>C.</td> <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Ni</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math></td> <td>iii.</td> <td>.<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></math></td> </tr> <tr> <td>D.</td> <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math></td> <td>iv.</td> <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></math></td> </tr> </tbody></table><p>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "dce945", "text": "(A)-I, (B)-II, (C)-III, (D)-IV", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466dd"}, {"id": "f83978", "text": "(A)-III, (B) - IV, (C) - I, (D)-II", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466de"}, {"id": "62d92e", "text": "(A)-IV, (B)-III, (C)-I, (D)-II", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466df"}, {"id": "30230a", "text": "(A)-II, (B)-I, (C)-IV, (D)-III", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466e0"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466e1", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f45"], "concepts": ["60e567ab499d1e49588f95dc"], "question": {"text": "The number of ions from the following that have the ability to liberate hydrogen from a dilute acid is _____.\n\\(\\mathrm{Ti}^{2+}, \\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}\\) and \\(\\mathrm{V}^{2+}\\)"}, "options": [{"id": "608362", "text": "2", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466e2"}, {"id": "62411e", "text": "3", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466e3"}, {"id": "734165", "text": "1", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466e4"}, {"id": "65df31", "text": "0", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f282ee0e063d92466e5"}], "questionLabels": []}], "markingScheme": {"_id": "608283e223cf4b3ad8f125e6", "title": "JEE Single Correct", "correctAttempt": 4, "incorrectAttempt": -1, "createdAt": "2021-04-09T08:45:27.797Z", "updatedAt": "2021-07-05T05:39:15.785Z", "__v": 0, "updated_by": "6042252509c5bf34e8a2f178"}, "maximumAttemptLimit": 20, "linkedQuestions": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T14:28:56.952Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-14T14:28:56.952Z", "__v": 0}, "position": 0, "_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d924673a"}, {"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d9246738", "title": "Chemistry Numerical", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d91", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d924671c", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f45"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52be"], "question": {"text": "<p>The number of statement(s) correct from the following for Copper is/are</p><p>(A) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cu</mi><mfenced><mi>II</mi></mfenced></math> complexes are always paramagnetic</p><p>(B) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cu</mi><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;complexes are generally colourless</p><p>(C) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cu</mi><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mfenced></math> is easily oxidized</p><p>(D) In Fehling solution, the active reagent has&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cu</mi><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mfenced></math></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "acbca5", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d924671d"}, {"id": "5b6d8e", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d924671e"}, {"id": "5d39b7", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d924671f"}, {"id": "c7914e", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d9246720"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d9246721", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb2066f9ce3f243b4b73"], "concepts": ["6162fbe76ed13040ec8545bb"], "question": {"text": "Consider the following reaction\n\\(\\mathrm{MnO}_2+\\mathrm{KOH}+\\mathrm{O}_2 \\rightarrow \\mathrm{A}+\\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{O}\\).\nProduct ' \\(\\mathrm{A}\\) ' in neutral or acidic medium disproportionate to give products ' \\(\\mathrm{B}\\) ' and ' \\(\\mathrm{C}\\) ' along with water. The sum of spin-only magnetic moment values of \\(\\mathrm{B}\\) and \\(\\mathrm{C}\\) is \\(\\qquad\\) BM. (nearest integer) (Given atomic number of \\(\\mathrm{Mn}\\) is 25)"}, "options": [{"id": "c94bad", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d9246722"}, {"id": "a4b986", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d9246723"}, {"id": "06e191", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d9246724"}, {"id": "7e118f", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d9246725"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d9246726", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f45"], "concepts": ["60e567ab499d1e49588f95dc"], "question": {"text": "A first row transition metal with highest enthalpy of atomisation, upon reaction with oxygen at high temperature forms oxides of formula \\(\\mathrm{M}_2 \\mathrm{O}_{\\mathrm{n}}\\) (where \\(\\mathrm{n}=3,4\\), 5). The 'spin-only' magnetic moment value of the amphoteric oxide from the above oxides is ______ \\(\\mathrm{BM}\\) (near integer)\n(Given atomic number: \\(\\mathrm{Sc}: 21, \\mathrm{Ti}: 22, \\mathrm{~V}: 23, \\mathrm{Cr}: 24, \\mathrm{Mn}: 25, \\mathrm{Fe}: 26, \\mathrm{Co}: 27\\), \\(\\mathrm{Ni}: 28, \\mathrm{Cu}: 29, \\mathrm{Zn}: 30\\))"}, "options": [{"id": "cdd4fb", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d9246727"}, {"id": "7810b2", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d9246728"}, {"id": "1993d0", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d9246729"}, {"id": "be788f", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d924672a"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d924672b", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb2066f9ce3f243b4b73"], "concepts": ["6162fbe76ed13040ec8545cc"], "question": {"text": "<p>The number of terminal oxygen atoms present in the product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> obtained from the following reaction is</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>FeCr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#8594;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Fe</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>&#8594;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>Na</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "8791e3", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d924672c"}, {"id": "e694b6", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d924672d"}, {"id": "367398", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d924672e"}, {"id": "aac208", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d924672f"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d9246730", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205c0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f45"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52be"], "question": {"text": "Among \\(\\mathrm{VO}_2^{+}, \\mathrm{MnO}_4^{-}\\)and \\(\\mathrm{Cr}_2 \\mathrm{O}_7^{2-}\\), the spin-only magnetic moment value of the species with least oxidising ability is ______ BM (Nearest integer).\n(Given atomic member \\(\\mathrm{V}=23, \\mathrm{Mn}=25, \\mathrm{Cr}=24\\))"}, "options": [{"id": "a0140f", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d9246731"}, {"id": "14c05b", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d9246732"}, {"id": "fef470", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d9246733"}, {"id": "49c921", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d9246734"}], "questionLabels": []}], "markingScheme": {"_id": "608283c623cf4b3ad8f125e5", "title": "JEE Numerical", "correctAttempt": 4, "incorrectAttempt": -1, "createdAt": "2021-04-09T08:45:27.797Z", "updatedAt": "2021-07-05T05:39:27.056Z", "__v": 0, "updated_by": "6042252509c5bf34e8a2f178"}, "maximumAttemptLimit": 5, "linkedQuestions": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T14:28:57.049Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-14T14:28:57.049Z", "__v": 0}, "position": 1, "_id": "69de4f292ee0e063d924673b"